evidence.text:
//! Running: verify the capsule against the world as it is now, check that the authorities it
//! records are the ones the host will ask, then ask the host to run the pathway. The host
//! re-offers the pathway, asks authority for each placement and admission for each step; this
//! module decides none of it, and a capsule carries no witness it could use instead.
//!
//! ```text
//! authenticate_requester    the requester is proved when the directory requires a proof (a credential)
//! Capsule::verify           the world the capsule rests on is still the world (Valid grants nothing)
//! compose_checked           the steps are still compatible under the obligations the caller holds
//! required authorities      what the capsule records covers what the host will ask
//! authorized_pathways       the requester may request every step (Thymora, reads only)
//! run_pathway               the host composes again, then per step asks Thymora, Porion, Metabion
//! ```
//!
//! Each stage is reported under its own name and no stage stands in for another: a verified
//! capsule is not an authorized pathway, an authorized pathway is not an admitted step, an
//! admitted step is not an executed one, and an executed one is not an observation until the host
//! has read it back.
//!
//! Who runs it is a statement until it is proved (DECISION 0157, PROVISIONAL). A capsule names the
//! principal it runs as (the `as` clause of its program), and a name proves nobody. A directory
//! whose operator set `auth-require` has said that no request may be taken on a name again, and the
//! node's command line and control socket keep that: a bare `--as` is refused and a socket line
//! must begin `auth TOKEN`. [`authenticate_requester`] is the same door for a capsule: before the
//! world is read it asks the host to authenticate the credential the caller presents, which must
//! prove the principal the capsule runs as, and it refuses a bare name when the directory requires
//! a credential. It decides nothing else: authentication grants nothing, and the requester still
//! acts only while Active and authorized, within the scope of the credential, which the host
//! applies from then on to every act of the run. [`run`] does not ask it again, because it takes a
//! host the caller has opened and has not been given a credential: the stage belongs to whoever
//! opens the host for a run (the command line, `cli::run_capsule`), and a caller that runs a
//! capsule without it runs it on a name.
//!
//! The command line executes only kernels it was given in a [`KernelTable`], which
//! [`KernelTable::production`] builds from the production kernels that exist, each with what it
//! asks of the body ([`TableDemand`] is the resource model Ontogen is given). The work a pathway
//! performs is a [`ProgramWork`]: where each step is placed comes from the program (the resources
//! it allows effects on and the instant it reads), and what each step runs comes from the table.
//! A step whose implementation has no kernel in the table is not placed, so it is not requested
//! and not run, and the refusal names it (DECISION 0125, Q8).
//!
//! A kernel answers bytes for the step after it. Most of the production kernels answer text
//! written to be read (the byte door of their module defines it), and the table enters those as
//! printing ([`KernelTable::register_printing`]): the work keeps what its last step answered, and
//! the command line prints it when every step of the pathway ran, met its contract and was read
//! back (DECISION 0146), and, under another label, when the pathway stopped at a step that ran and
//! did not meet its contract, so that the kernel's own account of why is not lost (DECISION 0149).
//! These printing outputs remain the output of an executed step, not an observation. The bounded
//! native BLAKE3 verification also supplies a digest-only result in its immutable work evidence;
//! the command line displays that result only after the host's authorized canonical read.
//!
//! Bytes pass from one step to the next as the work keeps them: the first chain that runs is a
//! feature transform and then the held-out comparison of least squares, where the frame the
//! transform answers is the frame the comparison reads (DECISION 0150). Which kernels are chained
//! is decided by the graph and its declarations and never here; the graph joins these two only by a
//! declaration that neither family makes for itself.
//!
//! One kernel answers bytes when its contract is met and text when it is not: the chart constructor
//! returns the canonical bytes of a specification, or the rules that its description breaks. The
//! table enters it as diagnosing ([`KernelTable::register_diagnosing`]): nothing of a met answer is
//! printed, as for a kernel entered plainly, and the text of a step that stopped the run is printed
//! under the label of a stopped step, as for a printing kernel (DECISION 0154, PROVISIONAL).

pub fn run<M: ResourceModel, W: PathwayWork>(
    host: &mut Host,
    capsule: &Capsule,
    graph: &GraphView,
    model: &M,
    contracts: &Contracts,
    work: &mut W,
    run_key: &str,
    reading: Reading,
) -> Result<PathwayRun, RunError> {
    let Some(requester) = capsule.ir().requester().cloned() else {
        return Err(RunError::NoRequester);
    };
    let pathway = capsule.selection().pathway.clone();
    let snapshot: Snapshot = graph.as_of(reading.valid, reading.known);
    {
        let world = HostWorld::new(&*host, graph);
        // VERIFY: the world the capsule rests on, read again.
        let verified = capsule.verify(&world, &snapshot, model, reading);
        let faults = world.faults();
        if !faults.is_empty() {
            return Err(RunError::World(faults));
        }
        if let Verification::Drifted(drifts) = verified {
            return Err(RunError::Drifted(drifts));
        }
        // OBLIGATIONS: the steps are still compatible under the contracts the caller holds.
        if let Some(goal) = capsule.ir().goal() {
            let checked = compose_checked(
                &snapshot,
                goal,
                model,
                &capsule.ir().selection_constraints(),
                contracts,
                &capsule.ir().facts(),
            )
            .map_err(RunError::FlowUndefined)?;
            if !checked.pathways.contains(&pathway) {
                if let Some(refused) = checked.refused.into_iter().find(|i| i.pathway == pathway) {
                    return Err(RunError::Incompatible(Box::new(refused)));
                }
            }
        }
        // AUTHORITIES: what the host will ask is covered by what the capsule recorded.
        let asked = world
            .asks(&requester, &pathway, &*work)
            .map_err(|e| match e {
                AsksError::Unplaced { step } => RunError::Unplaced { step },
                AsksError::Host(e) => RunError::Host(e),
            })?;
        let asked: Vec<(String, NodeId)> = asked.into_iter().collect();
        let missing = capsule.understated(&asked);
        if !missing.is_empty() {
            return Err(RunError::CapsuleUnderstatesAuthority { missing });
        }
        // AUTHORIZED: the requester may request every step (reads only).
        let authorized = host
            .authorized_pathways(&requester, std::slice::from_ref(&pathway), &*work)
            .map_err(RunError::Host)?;
        if let Some(withheld) = authorized.withheld.first() {
            return Err(RunError::Withheld(*withheld));
        }
    }
    // EXECUTED: the host composes again, then asks authority and admission for every step.
    let constraints = capsule.ir().selection_constraints();
    let offer = Offer {
        graph: &snapshot,
        model,
        constraints: &constraints,
    };
    host.run_pathway(&requester, &offer, &pathway, work, run_key)
        .map_err(RunError::Pathway)

/// What a kernel answers for one step.
#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct KernelAnswer {
    /// The bytes it produced; the next step is fed them.
    pub output: Vec<u8>,
    /// Whether the output meets the contract of the capability, judged by the entry and not by
    /// the host.
    pub met: bool,
}

/// One kernel the command line may run.
#[derive(Clone, Debug)]
pub struct KernelEntry {
    /// The capability the kernel realizes, by its name.
    pub capability: String,
    /// The kernel's name; the implementation that stands for it carries the same name.
    pub name: String,
    /// What running the kernel for one step asks of the body, by dimension: the amounts the table
    /// states for it, in the words of the host's declared quantities. It is the resource model
    /// Ontogen is given for an implementation of this name (an implementation with no entry has
    /// no demand and is refused), declared by the owner of the table and never measured.
    pub demand: &'static [(&'static str, u128)],
    /// Runs the kernel on the bytes of the step before and the program's facts, or says why it
    /// cannot (a kernel's own refusal is the step failing).
    pub run: fn(&[u8], &Facts) -> Result<KernelAnswer, String>,
}

/// Why a kernel was not entered in a table.
#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum TableError {
    /// A kernel of this name is already in the table.
    Duplicate(String),
}

/// The kernels the command line executes, by name. An implementation the graph offers is run
/// only if a kernel of its name, realizing its capability, is in the table: the table is the
/// whole of what `run` can execute.

/// What one BLAKE3-256 verification asks of the body: one `cpu_units`. PROVISIONAL, awaiting the
/// owner's ratification (DECISION 0129): the amount is a declared unit cost, the one the suite has
/// always used for this kernel, and not a measurement; `cpu_units` is the quantity the host
/// declares for a resource of type `cpu`. A measured cost is a change of this constant, or of
/// where a demand is read from, and of nothing else.
pub const VERIFY_DEMAND: [(&str, u128); 1] = [("cpu_units", 1)];

/// The production BLAKE3-256 verification: the input is hashed and set beside the digest the
/// program claims (the fact `claimed`, 64 hexadecimal digits). Nothing claimed verifies nothing,
/// so the contract is not met. The kernels crate's registry selects the kernel, so its own
/// requirement (the input size) is the registry's refusal.
fn verify_blake3(input: &[u8], facts: &Facts) -> Result<KernelAnswer, String> {
    let claimed = facts
        .text("claimed")
        .and_then(unhex)
        .and_then(|bytes| <[u8; 32]>::try_from(bytes).ok());
    let request = DigestRequest { input, claimed };
    let mut registry = KernelRegistry::new();
    blake3::register(&mut registry).map_err(|e| e.to_string())?;
    let capability =
        SemanticAction::try_from(blake3::VERIFY_CAPABILITY).map_err(|e| format!("{e:?}"))?;
    let bound = registry
        .select_named(&capability, blake3::VERIFY_KERNEL, &request)
        .map_err(|e| e.to_string())?;
    let check = bound.run(&request);
    Ok(KernelAnswer {
        output: check.computed.to_vec(),
        met: check.matches() == Some(true),
    })

/// A bounded native BLAKE3 verification result. The input itself is never exposed in evidence.
/// This is a provider payload inside the existing work receipt, not an authority contract.
#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct Blake3Result {
    pub input_sha256: [u8; 32],
    pub ir_digest: [u8; 32],
    pub claimed: Option<[u8; 32]>,
    pub computed: [u8; 32],
    pub matched: bool,
}

impl Blake3Result {
    pub fn to_bytes(&self) -> Vec<u8> {
        let mut out = Writer::new();
        out.u16(1)
            .str("codara.blake3.verification")
            .bytes(&self.input_sha256)
            .bytes(&self.ir_digest);
        match self.claimed {
            Some(claimed) => {
                out.u8(1).bytes(&claimed);
            }
            None => {
                out.u8(0);
            }
        }
        out.bytes(&self.computed).u8(u8::from(self.matched));
        out.finish()
    }

    pub fn from_bytes(bytes: &[u8]) -> Result<Self, DecodeError> {
        let invalid = |field| DecodeError::Invalid {
            field,
            reason: "invalid native verification result".to_owned(),
        };
        if bytes.len() > 1024 {
            return Err(invalid("result size"));
        }
        let mut input = Reader::new(bytes);
        let version = input.u16()?;
        if version != 1 {
            return Err(DecodeError::UnsupportedVersion(version));
        }
        if input.str()? != "codara.blake3.verification" {
            return Err(invalid("result kind"));
        }
        let input_sha256 = input
            .bytes()?
            .try_into()
            .map_err(|_| invalid("input digest"))?;
        let ir_digest = input
            .bytes()?
            .try_into()
            .map_err(|_| invalid("IR digest"))?;
        let claimed = match input.u8()? {
            0 => None,
            1 => Some(
                input
                    .bytes()?
                    .try_into()
                    .map_err(|_| invalid("claimed digest"))?,
            ),
            tag => {
                return Err(DecodeError::UnknownTag {
                    field: "claim",
                    tag,
                })
            }
        };
        let computed = input
            .bytes()?
            .try_into()
            .map_err(|_| invalid("computed digest"))?;
        let matched = match input.u8()? {
            0 => false,
            1 => true,
            tag => {
                return Err(DecodeError::UnknownTag {
                    field: "matched",
                    tag,
                })
            }
        };
        input.finish()?;
        if matched != (claimed == Some(computed)) {
            return Err(invalid("verdict"));
        }
        Ok(Self {
            input_sha256,
            ir_digest,
            claimed,
            computed,
            matched,
        })
    }
}

/// Compact evidence for strict historical FUNCTIONS validation, never the decoded record tables.
/// Missing digests remain missing when refusal happened before their recomputation.
#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct FunctionsResult {
    pub object_sha256: [u8; 32],
    pub ir_digest: [u8; 32],
    pub file_name_sha256: [u8; 32],
    pub expected: Option<[u8; 32]>,
    pub computed: Option<[u8; 32]>,
    pub verified: bool,
    pub first_defect: Option<Defect>,

pub fn from_bytes(bytes: &[u8]) -> Result<Self, DecodeError> {
    let invalid = |field| DecodeError::Invalid {
        field,
        reason: "invalid historical FUNCTIONS result".into(),
    };
    if bytes.len() > 1024 {
        return Err(invalid("result size"));
    }
    let mut input = Reader::new(bytes);
    let version = input.u16()?;

/// The work of a program: `ir` says where steps may be placed and from when; `snapshot` is the
/// graph the pathway was composed over, which names the capabilities and implementations the
/// table is matched against; `payload` is what the first step is fed.
pub fn new(
    ir: &SemanticIr,
    snapshot: &Snapshot,
    table: &KernelTable,
    payload: Vec<u8>,
) -> ProgramWork {

impl PathwayWork for ProgramWork {
    fn request_binding(&self, _index: usize, step: &Step) -> Option<RequestDigest> {
        let kernel = self.kernels.get(&step.implementation)?;
        let domain: &[u8] = if kernel.name == blake3::VERIFY_KERNEL
            && kernel.capability == blake3::VERIFY_CAPABILITY
        {
            b"codara.blake3.verification.v1"
        } else if kernel.name == legacy_artifact::KERNEL_FUNCTIONS_OBJECT
            && kernel.capability == legacy_artifact::VERIFY_CAPABILITY
        {
            b"codara.legacy.functions.v1"
        } else if kernel.name == price::PRICE_KERNEL && kernel.capability == price::PRICE_CAPABILITY
        {
            b"codara.commerce.price.parse-exact.v1"
        } else if kernel.name == price_text::PRICE_TEXT_KERNEL
            && kernel.capability == price_text::PRICE_TEXT_CAPABILITY
        {
            b"codara.commerce.price.parse-text.v1"
        } else {
            return None;
        };
        Some(RequestDigest::of(&[
            domain,
            &self.ir_digest,
            step.implementation.to_string().as_bytes(),
            step.capability.to_string().as_bytes(),
            kernel.name.as_bytes(),
            &sha256(&self.carry),
        ]))
    }

    fn outcome_evidence(&self, index: usize, _step: &Step) -> Option<Vec<u8>> {
        self.native_results
            .get(&index)
            .map(Blake3Result::to_bytes)
            .or_else(|| {
                self.functions_results
                    .get(&index)
                    .map(FunctionsResult::to_bytes)
            })
            .or_else(|| self.price_results.get(&index).map(PriceResult::to_bytes))
            .or_else(|| {
                self.price_text_results
                    .get(&index)
                    .map(PriceTextResult::to_bytes)
            })

fn perform(
    &mut self,
    index: usize,
    step: &Step,
    _effect: &EffectRef,
    _lease: &world::lease::Lease,
) -> Result<WorkReport, String> {

} else if kernel.name == legacy_artifact::KERNEL_FUNCTIONS_OBJECT
    && kernel.capability == legacy_artifact::VERIFY_CAPABILITY
{
    let (answer, verdict) = functions_answer(&self.carry, &self.facts)?;
    let file_name = self
        .facts
        .text("file_name")
        .expect("functions_answer required file_name");
    let result = FunctionsResult {
        object_sha256: sha256(&self.carry),
        ir_digest: self.ir_digest,
        file_name_sha256: sha256(file_name.as_bytes()),
        expected: verdict.expected.map(|digest| *digest.bytes()),
        computed: verdict.computed.map(|digest| *digest.bytes()),
        verified: verdict.is_verified(),
        first_defect: verdict.refusals().first().map(|(defect, _)| *defect),
    };
    FunctionsResult::from_bytes(&result.to_bytes()).map_err(|error| error.to_string())?;
    self.functions_results.insert(index, result);
    answer
} else {
    (kernel.run)(&self.carry, &self.facts)?
};
let met = answer.met;
if kernel.name == blake3::VERIFY_KERNEL && kernel.capability == blake3::VERIFY_CAPABILITY {
    let computed = <[u8; 32]>::try_from(answer.output.as_slice())
        .map_err(|_| "the BLAKE3 kernel returned no 32-byte digest".to_owned())?;
    let claimed = self
        .facts
        .text("claimed")
        .and_then(unhex)
        .and_then(|bytes| <[u8; 32]>::try_from(bytes).ok());
    if met != (claimed == Some(computed)) {
        return Err("the BLAKE3 kernel verdict contradicts its digest".to_owned());
    }
    self.native_results.insert(
        index,
        Blake3Result {
            input_sha256: sha256(&self.carry),
            ir_digest: self.ir_digest,
            claimed,
            computed,
            matched: met,
        },
    );
}
self.carry = answer.output;
self.last = Some(Performed {
    step: index,
    kernel: kernel.name.clone(),
    diagnosing: self.diagnosing.contains(&step.implementation),
    printing: self.printing.contains(&step.implementation),
    met,
});
Ok(WorkReport::known(consumed, met))

    }
}

/// The most bytes of a kernel's text that `run` prints. PROVISIONAL, awaiting the owner (DECISION
/// 0146): large enough for the summary, a quantile and a fit over some tens of columns (about
/// 250 bytes a column), small enough that one run cannot flood a terminal or a log.
pub const OUTPUT_LIMIT: usize = 16 * 1024;

let read = |path: &PathBuf| {
    std::fs::read(path).map_err(|e| format!("codara: cannot read {}: {e}\n", path.display()))
};
let source = match read(&invocation.file) {
    Ok(bytes) => bytes,
    Err(message) => return failure(message),
};
let payload = match &invocation.payload {
    Some(path) => match read(path) {
        Ok(bytes) => bytes,
        Err(message) => return failure(message),
    },
    None => Vec::new(),

fn run_capsule(
    invocation: &Invocation,
    source: &[u8],
    world: World<'_>,
    setting: &Setting<'_>,
) -> Outcome {
    let mut report = Report::new();
    report.line("codara run");
    let capsule = match Capsule::from_bytes(source) {
        Ok(capsule) => capsule,
        Err(error) => {
            report.line(format_args!(
                "stage capsule: refused: not a capsule: {error:?}"
            ));
            return report.end(exit::REFUSED);
        }
    };
    report.line(format_args!(
        "stage capsule: read: {}, meaning {}",
        capsule.digest(),
        capsule.semantic_digest()
    ));
    // AUTHENTICATION: the principal the capsule runs as is a name until it is proved. This is the
    // one place the command line opens the host for a run and knows who the run is for, so it is
    // asked here, before the world is read and before anything is requested; a capsule that names
    // no principal is refused further on, by the run, before anything is requested either.
    if let Some(requester) = capsule.ir().requester() {
        let presented = invocation.token.as_ref().map(Presented::token);
        match run::authenticate_requester(&mut *world.host, requester, presented, setting.clock) {
            Ok(Some(proven)) => report.line(format_args!(
                "stage authentication: ok: the credential proves {proven} (authentication grants nothing: authority and admission are asked next)"
            )),
            Ok(None) => {}
            Err(refusal) => {
                report.line(format_args!(
                    "stage authentication: refused: {refusal}; nothing was verified, nothing was requested"
                ));
                report.line(format_args!("ir digest: {}", capsule.semantic_digest()));
                return report.end(exit::NOT_AUTHENTICATED);
            }

let model = Model(setting.demand);
let mut work = ProgramWork::new(
    capsule.ir(),
    &snapshot,
    setting.kernels,
    setting.payload.clone(),
)
.at_execution(setting.clock);
let run_key = invocation
    .run_key
    .clone()
    .unwrap_or_else(|| default_run_key(&capsule));
let semantic = capsule.semantic_digest();
let World { host, graph, .. } = world;
let result = run::run(
    host,
    &capsule,
    graph,
    &model,
    setting.contracts,
    &mut work,
    &run_key,
    reading,
);

// Use the resource and request key the host actually executed, and authority now,
// rather than the program's historical reading or a predicted placement.
let Some(requester) = capsule.ir().requester() else {
    continue;
};
let observed = match host.work_evidence(
    requester,
    &step.resource,
    &step.work,
    setting.clock,
) {
    // A requester without observation rights retains the original execution
    // report; receiving a verification result requires its own existing right.
    Err(HostError::Unauthorized { .. }) => continue,
    other => other.ok().flatten(),
};
let saved_bytes = observed.and_then(|evidence| match evidence.proof {
    lineara::record::EvidenceProof::ProviderAck(receipt) => {
        lineara::work_receipt::result(&receipt).ok().flatten()
    }
    lineara::record::EvidenceProof::Unobserved => None,
});

if let Some(expected) = functions_expected {
    let saved = saved_bytes
        .as_deref()
        .and_then(|bytes| run::FunctionsResult::from_bytes(bytes).ok());
    match saved {
        Some(saved) if &saved == expected => report.line(format_args!(
            "historical FUNCTIONS result: persisted canonical evidence; step {}; resource {}; key {}; object sha256:{}; verified {}; first_defect {}; expected blake3:{}; computed blake3:{}",
            step.index, step.resource, step.work,
            genora_primitives::digest::hex(&saved.object_sha256), saved.verified,
            saved.first_defect.map(|defect| defect.code()).unwrap_or("none"),
            saved.expected.map(|digest| genora_primitives::digest::hex(&digest)).unwrap_or_else(|| "not computed".to_owned()),
            saved.computed.map(|digest| genora_primitives::digest::hex(&digest)).unwrap_or_else(|| "not computed".to_owned()),
        )),
        _ => report.line(format_args!("historical FUNCTIONS result: unavailable or inconsistent at step {}; no persisted result claimed", step.index)),
    }
    continue;
}
let saved = saved_bytes
    .as_deref()
    .and_then(|bytes| run::Blake3Result::from_bytes(bytes).ok());
match saved {
    Some(saved) if Some(&saved) == blake_expected => report.line(format_args!(
        "native verification result: persisted canonical evidence; step {}; resource {}; key {}; input sha256:{}; computed blake3:{}; matched {}",
        step.index, step.resource, step.work,
        genora_primitives::digest::hex(&saved.input_sha256),
        genora_primitives::digest::hex(&saved.computed), saved.matched
    )),
    _ => {
        report.line(format_args!("native verification result: unavailable or inconsistent at step {}; no persisted result claimed", step.index));
    }
}

report.line(format_args!(
    "{opening}: {} bytes, {} printed, {} truncated, {} control characters escaped (limit {})",
    bytes.len(),
    shown.len(),
    bytes.len() - shown.len(),
    escaped,
    OUTPUT_LIMIT
));
let produced = match door {
    Door::Completed => "produced by the executed step",
    Door::Stopped => {
        "produced by the executed step, which did not meet its contract and ended the run there"
    }
};
report.line(format_args!(
    "kernel output status: {produced}, not read back from canonical history (the observation covers consumption and completion, not these bytes); not an authorization and not a claim that the output is correct"
));

//! `codara` -- the Codara command line.
//!
//! ```text
//! codara check|graph|plan|prove|run|build [--data DIR] [--time VALID --known TRANSACTION] FILE
//! codara declarations [FAMILY...]
//! ```
//!
//! Exit codes: 0 done, 1 usage, 2 refused (something does not resolve), 3 not offered or withheld,
//! 4 refused by authority or admission, 5 the work ran and did not meet its contract or could not be
//! read back, 6 the requester is not authenticated.

use codara::cli;
use std::io::Write;
use std::process::ExitCode;

fn main() -> ExitCode {
    let args: Vec<String> = std::env::args().skip(1).collect();
    let outcome = cli::execute(&args);
    // A closed pipe is the reader's choice, not an error of this command.
    let _ = std::io::stdout().write_all(outcome.stdout.as_bytes());
    let _ = std::io::stderr().write_all(outcome.stderr.as_bytes());
    ExitCode::from(outcome.exit)
}

//! Native lease-work receipts carried by the existing opaque provider acknowledgement.
//!
//! Historical receipts retain their bytes and unknown outcomes. A provider may additionally
//! preserve a bounded result under an explicit version; readers validate the complete receipt
//! before trusting either its report or its result. This does not change evidence log framing,
//! resource accounting, or the shared work-report contract.

use genora_primitives::digest::{hex, unhex};
use metabion::lease_work::WorkReport;
use metabion::work_run::EffectRef;
use std::fmt;

/// Maximum opaque result size, independent of a resource's admitted work budget.
pub const MAX_RESULT_BYTES: usize = 1024;

const WORK_MARK: &str = " · work ";
const RESULT_MARK: &str = " · ";
const RESULT_VERSION: &str = "result1 ";

/// Encodes the report without changing historical bytes when no result was observed.
pub fn encode(
    provider: &str,
    effect: &EffectRef,
    lease: &str,
    report: &WorkReport,
    result: Option<&[u8]>,
) -> Result<String, ReceiptError> {
    if let Some(bytes) = result {
        check_size(bytes.len())?;
    }
    let part = |v: Option<String>| v.unwrap_or_else(|| UNKNOWN.into());
    let mut receipt = format!(
        "{provider} ran effect {effect} under lease {lease}{WORK_MARK}{} {}",
        part(report.consumed.map(|c| c.to_string())),
        part(report.completed.map(|c| c.to_string()))
    );
    if let Some(bytes) = result {
        receipt.push_str(RESULT_MARK);
        receipt.push_str(RESULT_VERSION);
        receipt.push_str(&hex(bytes));
    }
    Ok(receipt)
}

/// Reads a report only when the complete receipt, including any result, is supported and valid.
pub fn report(receipt: &str) -> Option<WorkReport> {
    parse(receipt).ok().map(|(report, _)| report)
}

/// Reads preserved result bytes; a historical receipt has no result, never an invented one.
pub fn result(receipt: &str) -> Result<Option<Vec<u8>>, ReceiptError> {
    parse(receipt).map(|(_, result)| result)
}

