//!   the step goes to the best placement where the requester holds a witness of an `Allow` for
//!   both `resource.lease` and `resource.work`, and admission of the work is asked before the
//!   lease is, so a lease is not taken for work that would be refused. A lease is an allocation
//!   and not the admission to work; here the two are asked together.
//! - The first refusal ends the run, as `NorlRuntime::execute_plan` ends a plan of Norl's own. So
//!   does a step whose work ran and did not meet its contract (`completed` is not `Some(true)`),
//!   and so does a step whose effect canonical history does not show. No step after it is
//!   requested. An absent durable Host run retains the legacy release behavior
//!   (`resource.release`, governed like everything else). Absence is only local record absence:
//!   an unbound legacy provider may have acted before its snapshot, so release is not proof of
//!   nonexecution. Pending work retains its allocation, including when its outcome is unknown;
//!   lookup uncertainty also leaves the lease held ([`Stranded`]). Lease expiry remains unchanged,
//!   so this does not establish indefinite economic reservation custody.
//! - Each step that ran is read back from canonical history ([`Host::observe_step`]): the lease is
//!   settled, the provider's evidence exists and names the lease, the settlement is canonical
//!   under this request's own key, and what the run reported is what the settlement says. What it
//!   returns is the only way to hold a [`StepObservation`], and its [`basis`](StepObservation::basis)
//!   is the host's to give, not the caller's.
//!
//! Not claimed. A run key names one run: reusing it is refused, so a pathway is not resumed after
//! a crash (each of its requests is idempotent by key; resuming the sequence is not built). The
//! host cannot tell a simulator from a real provider by what it does: the simulated work provider
//! ([`SIMULATED_WORK`]) is known by its name, and a real provider that lies about being one is the
//! membrane's concern. `work_under_lease` itself does not ask whether the lease is still live when
//! it runs the work (see DECISION 0116, defects found); a pathway always runs its work under the
//! lease it has just taken.

/// What the caller knows of its steps that the capability graph does not: where each is placed and
/// what performing it does.
pub trait PathwayWork {
    /// Where step `index` of the pathway is requested; `None` when the caller cannot place it
    /// (nothing is then requested, and the run stops there).
    fn placement(&self, index: usize, step: &Step) -> Option<StepPlacement>;

    /// Optional immutable input identity for native work. Existing providers retain their
    /// historical request identity when they return None; an identity grants no authority.
    fn request_binding(
        &self,
        _index: usize,
        _step: &Step,
    ) -> Option<metabion::work_run::RequestDigest> {
        None
    }

    /// Bounded opaque result evidence of the step actually performed, never a prediction.
    fn outcome_evidence(&self, _index: usize, _step: &Step) -> Option<Vec<u8>> {
        None
    }

    /// Performs the work of step `index` under the lease it holds and reports what it consumed and
    /// whether it met its contract (`completed`). An `Err` is a failure to run at all.
    fn perform(
        &mut self,
        index: usize,
        step: &Step,
        effect: &EffectRef,
        lease: &world::lease::Lease,
    ) -> Result<WorkReport, String>;
}

