//! Codara, Chronica's graph-native semantic intent language, as a pure library.
//!
//! A program states what must become true, what must remain true, what may change, what
//! evidence is required and what constraints govern the transformation. This crate turns that
//! text into a *candidate*: it parses and types the surface, resolves every name against a
//! bitemporal reading of the capability graph, lowers the result to a semantic IR with a
//! canonical encoding, states the proof obligations, and seals a capsule that records which
//! pathway was selected and what is still open. It decides nothing about what may happen.
//!
//! The chain it keeps apart: Source != Meaning (the IR) != Plan (a pathway) != Authority !=
//! Admission != Execution != Observation != Truth. Compiled != Authorized; a valid program is not
//! admitted now; a capsule is not authority; an AST is not the source of truth; the IR is not a
//! second graph store.
//!
//! The language owner resides at `codara/` and is pure: no I/O, no clock, no randomness,
//! no threads, floats nowhere. Its manifest names the genome, the world and the capability graph
//! and nothing that authorizes, admits, records evidence or executes, so a compiled program
//! cannot name an authority witness (a test of this crate pins that no source file ever does).
//!
//! A name in a program is data, never an identity: resolution reads what the graph holds and
//! mints nothing. The only value that refers to a node is a [`ir::HeldRef`], built from a node the
//! reading holds.

pub mod capsule;
pub mod encode;
pub mod ir;
pub mod lower;
pub mod prove;
pub mod refusal;
pub mod resolve;

